169
00:21:13,438 --> 00:21:19,186
Alex: More changes, fewer rollbacks, and no line-by-line review. How did they do it?

170
00:21:19,643 --> 00:21:21,914
Maya: He listed five specific practices.

171
00:21:21,914 --> 00:21:28,725
Maya: Practice one: before submitting a change, have a second assistant review it with fresh eyes.

172
00:21:29,351 --> 00:21:31,510
Alex: Okay. What is practice two?

173
00:21:32,053 --> 00:21:41,380
Maya: Practice two: if the exact same review comment is given twice, write it into a permanent REVIEW file so the next review checks for it automatically.

174
00:21:41,939 --> 00:21:47,187
Alex: Say a correction twice, and it becomes a written rule in the review file.

175
00:21:47,187 --> 00:21:49,811
Alex: What are practices three, four, and five?

176
00:21:50,325 --> 00:21:59,269
Maya: Practice three: check small, low-risk changes quickly, and spend deep review time on changes where a mistake would cost money.

177
00:21:59,269 --> 00:22:11,194
Maya: Practice four: anything touching login, payments, or public release must always be approved by a human—a bot's approval can never replace a human's approval there.

178
00:22:11,194 --> 00:22:22,267
Maya: And practice five: put new changes behind a feature toggle to test internally first, roll them out gradually, and always keep a one-step rollback ready.

179
00:22:22,968 --> 00:22:37,335
Maya: 也就是说：同一条审查意见只要说了两次，就立刻写进规则文件下次自动查；而凡是涉及花钱和对外发布的事，永远必须由真人批准。

180
00:22:37,790 --> 00:22:46,412
Alex: Now remember our hard rule: Fisher does not write code, and we never touch code details when Fisher writes or replies.

181
00:22:46,412 --> 00:22:49,697
Alex: Why is this story still useful for Fisher?

182
00:22:50,221 --> 00:23:09,642
Maya: Because the two rules that have nothing to do with code—"if you say the same feedback twice, write it into the rule file" and "anything that costs money or goes public requires human approval"—are the exact way Fisher builds his own rules folder in library item D R oh five and E V oh eight!

183
00:23:10,098 --> 00:23:35,819
Alex: For a writing angle, the daily notes suggest using D R oh five and E V oh eight around the theme: "How I set rules for my A I assistants: if I have to correct the same mistake twice, it becomes a written rule." And we have a reply draft to Prajwal in Segment four that focuses strictly on the non-code rule-making side.

184
00:23:36,293 --> 00:23:46,373
Maya: So the takeaway is: never repeat the same correction three times—the second time a bot makes the same mistake, lock the fix into its checklist.

185
00:23:47,717 --> 00:23:57,321
Maya: 一句话：同一个错只要纠正了两次，就把它写成书面规矩，下次让审核助手自动对照检查。

186
00:23:59,371 --> 00:24:08,882
Alex: Next up is story five, from E X M seven seven seven seven, who has about one hundred fifty-three thousand followers.

187
00:24:08,882 --> 00:24:14,503
Alex: Yesterday we covered his seven rules for making Claude write like a human.

188
00:24:14,503 --> 00:24:17,530
Alex: Today he posted two more workflow gems.

189
00:24:18,111 --> 00:24:27,192
Maya: In his first post, he talked about how every single week there is a brand-new "frontier model" release, and everyone obsesses over benchmark scores.

190
00:24:27,192 --> 00:24:33,730
Maya: He said the only thing that actually matters is how the model fits into your own real work.

191
00:24:34,266 --> 00:24:37,676
Alex: How does he test whether a new model is worth switching to?

192
00:24:38,231 --> 00:24:44,548
Maya: He uses a simple three-step test. First: throw your own real daily tasks at it.

193
00:24:44,548 --> 00:24:52,443
Maya: Second: look for three to five specific places where it is noticeably better than the model you use right now.

194
00:24:52,443 --> 00:24:59,549
Maya: And third: keep pushing it harder and harder until it breaks, so you know where its ceiling is.

195
00:25:00,174 --> 00:25:01,842
Alex: And what was in his second post?

196
00:25:02,361 --> 00:25:10,988
Maya: He shared a workflow audit prompt—a prompt that tells the A I assistant to interview you about how you work before it suggests any automation.

197
00:25:10,988 --> 00:25:20,941
Maya: And look at the constraint he put inside the prompt: "Ask only two to three questions at a time, and do not move to the next step until I confirm."

198
00:25:21,509 --> 00:25:29,469
Alex: There is that human-in-the-loop pattern again: ask two or three questions, wait for my confirmation, then proceed.

199
00:25:29,469 --> 00:25:35,438
Alex: And look at his first post about testing models on real tasks instead of benchmarks.

200
00:25:36,025 --> 00:25:51,510
Maya: That is practically the exact answer to Fisher's library item W M P six b—how to judge whether a new A I tool is worth your time—and library item X oh seven, which is about assigning different models by task complexity!

201
00:25:52,031 --> 00:25:52,460
Alex: Right!

202
00:25:52,460 --> 00:26:02,065
Alex: In Fisher's setup, as documented in his model routing rules from October sixteenth, Argon writes the drafts while Grok reads X and searches.

203
00:26:02,065 --> 00:26:13,672
Alex: That split wasn't decided by reading public benchmark charts—it came from running them on Fisher's actual daily tasks and seeing which one did each job better.

204
00:26:14,059 --> 00:26:25,880
Maya: 简单说：新模型值不值得换，别看网上的跑分榜，直接拿自己每天干的真活去试，按实际表现给不同模型分工。

205
00:26:26,483 --> 00:26:44,691
Alex: The daily notes mark this as today's backup idea for an original post: "I don't look at benchmarks; I look at which specific task it does better than what I have now." And we also have a direct reply draft to E X M seven seven seven seven in Segment four.

206
00:26:45,323 --> 00:26:53,550
Maya: So the takeaway is: ignore the weekly benchmark hype and assign models to jobs based on testing them on your own daily work.

207
00:26:54,730 --> 00:27:04,770
Maya: 一句话：别看跑分看疗效，拿自己的日常任务去实测，哪个模型在哪件活上更强就让它干哪件。

208
00:27:06,820 --> 00:27:17,442
Alex: Let's close out Segment two with story eleven, a Chinese post from Shanghai by an account called vikingmute, who has about sixty-nine thousand followers.

209
00:27:18,079 --> 00:27:32,953
Maya: He posted that he connected a third-party M C P—Model Context Protocol connector—for Google Search Console to his Grok Bot, and handed total control of his website's S E O over to the bot!

210
00:27:33,404 --> 00:27:36,798
Alex: Wait—total control? What did he tell the bot to do?

211
00:27:37,399 --> 00:27:48,111
Maya: He set it up on a weekly schedule to observe the search data, spot changes, make a plan, modify the pages, and push the changes live to the website.

212
00:27:48,111 --> 00:28:03,627
Maya: In his words, roughly: "Just tell me after it's live." He also noted that a Google Search Console connector is a very practical need, and even though there is no official version yet, there are already plenty of third-party options.

213
00:28:03,627 --> 00:28:05,843
Maya: His post got twenty-one bookmarks.

214
00:28:06,455 --> 00:28:07,899
Alex: Okay, pause right there!

215
00:28:07,899 --> 00:28:16,921
Alex: "Modify the pages, push them live, and just tell me after it's done." That is the exact opposite of how Fisher runs his team.

216
00:28:17,398 --> 00:28:18,582
Maya: It really is!

217
00:28:18,582 --> 00:28:26,475
Maya: Now, having a bot automatically pull Google Search Console data every week, watch for changes, and draft an action plan?

218
00:28:26,475 --> 00:28:32,789
Maya: That part is super useful for Fisher's G E O and S E O work.

219
00:28:33,323 --> 00:28:37,571
Alex: Totally. Automating data observation saves a ton of time.

220
00:28:37,571 --> 00:28:41,819
Alex: Where do Fisher and vikingmute draw the line differently?

221
00:28:42,432 --> 00:28:46,676
Maya: On pushing changes straight to a live website without a human check.

222
00:28:46,676 --> 00:28:51,982
Maya: Fisher's team rule is that assistants only write drafts, and Fisher sends things himself.

223
00:28:51,982 --> 00:28:58,702
Maya: Whether he also keeps a human check before live page changes is one of today's things to confirm.

224
00:28:58,702 --> 00:29:01,885
Maya: We'll come back to it in segment four.

225
00:29:02,438 --> 00:29:15,039
Maya: 也就是说：让 Bot 每周自动看 Search Console 数据、出计划非常省心，但直接修改上线这一步，留一道真人把关会踏实得多。

226
00:29:15,615 --> 00:29:31,141
Alex: The daily notes say no standalone post is needed here, and instead recommend using today's one daily Chinese reply slot to chat constructively with vikingmute about where each person draws that boundary.

227
00:29:31,750 --> 00:29:40,856
Maya: So the takeaway is: let bots automate all the search data monitoring you want, and keep a human check right before live pages change.

228
00:29:42,108 --> 00:29:50,028
Maya: 一句话：数据观察和出方案可以全自动，改线上页面留一步人看最稳妥。

229
00:29:52,078 --> 00:29:52,880
Maya: Quick break.

230
00:29:52,880 --> 00:30:18,552
Maya: When we come back: Peter Yang on X's worsening reply-bot problem and why big-company resumes can be a "car wreck" in fast teams, Riley Brown's fifteen-step video edit from a single prompt, borrowing old tricks from other industries, why nobody in tech feels optimistic despite seeing fifty huge opportunities, and the ad K P I behind annoying splash screens.

231
00:30:19,247 --> 00:30:33,344
Maya: 休息一下，马上回来聊怎么在满屏机器人回复里写出真人感、一句话剪完视频的十五个步骤，以及几个关于增长与行业心态的观察。

232
00:30:36,894 --> 00:30:43,467
Alex: Welcome back. Segment three. Growth mindset, A I media, and quick hits.

233
00:30:43,962 --> 00:30:51,297
Maya: Let's start Segment three with story seven, from Peter Yang, who has about two hundred eighty-nine thousand followers.

234
00:30:51,297 --> 00:30:54,598
Maya: He had two notable posts in the feed today.

235
00:30:55,088 --> 00:30:58,195
Alex: His first post got seven hundred ten likes.

236
00:30:58,195 --> 00:31:07,129
Alex: He said the reply-bot problem on X has gotten noticeably worse—and Elon Musk actually replied to him saying, "Working on it."

237
00:31:07,652 --> 00:31:15,767
Maya: And in his second post, Peter shared a clip from his newest podcast episode with Pomp, handle A Pompliano.

238
00:31:15,767 --> 00:31:20,038
Maya: Pomp talked about hiring people with impressive big-company resumes.

239
00:31:20,524 --> 00:31:26,573
Alex: How did Pomp describe hiring big-company people into a small, fast-moving team?

240
00:31:27,093 --> 00:31:29,683
Maya: He said it was "like a car wreck"!

241
00:31:29,683 --> 00:31:34,864
Maya: Because people from giant companies are used to having lots of resources and long, slow processes.

242
00:31:34,864 --> 00:31:39,721
Maya: Then you drop them into a team that has an idea at nine a.m.

243
00:31:39,721 --> 00:31:42,312
Maya: and ships it by ten thirty a.m.

244
00:31:42,312 --> 00:31:52,350
Maya: Pomp also mentioned that people ask A I financial questions they are too embarrassed to ask a human financial advisor, because they aren't afraid of the A I judging them.

245
00:31:52,990 --> 00:31:58,745
Alex: Let's zoom in on Peter's first post about X being flooded with reply bots.

246
00:31:58,745 --> 00:32:02,342
Alex: Why does that matter directly to Fisher's daily workflow?

247
00:32:02,907 --> 00:32:09,131
Maya: Because Fisher uses daily replies to build relationships and grow his presence on X!

248
00:32:09,131 --> 00:32:26,912
Maya: When the platform is flooded with automated reply bots, two things happen at once: first, a thoughtful reply that sounds like a real person stands out much more; second, everyone is suspicious, so generic replies immediately get dismissed as bots.

249
00:32:27,430 --> 00:32:32,819
Alex: So how do you prove in two sentences that you are a real practitioner and not a reply bot?

250
00:32:33,323 --> 00:32:42,301
Maya: By including a specific, lived detail from your own work this week—something a bot could never guess or make up from reading the original post!

251
00:32:43,042 --> 00:32:55,996
Maya: 简单说：X 上的机器人回复越多，越不能说空话；在回复里带上一句只有你自己干过才知道的细节，比任何蓝标认证都管用。

252
00:32:56,535 --> 00:33:10,972
Alex: The daily notes say skip writing a standalone post on this, read the Pomp note as background, and reply to Peter's post about reply bots—without mentioning that Fisher uses assistants to draft his replies.

253
00:33:11,618 --> 00:33:20,080
Maya: So the takeaway is: beat the reply-bot noise by anchoring every reply in a real detail from what you actually built or tested.

254
00:33:21,333 --> 00:33:30,603
Maya: 一句话：机器人能模仿语气但编不出你这周真干了什么，真细节就是最好的真人签名。

255
00:33:32,653 --> 00:33:37,718
Alex: Story eight is a wild look at where A I video editing is heading.

256
00:33:37,718 --> 00:33:46,401
Alex: It comes from Riley Brown in Brooklyn, who has about two hundred fifty-seven thousand followers, paired with a quick reaction from Peter Yang.

257
00:33:46,956 --> 00:34:00,177
Maya: Riley posted a video with the caption: "This video was edited with one prompt." The post got one hundred thirty-one bookmarks, and Peter Yang commented that Claude is insanely good at video editing.

258
00:34:00,806 --> 00:34:04,774
Alex: Hold on—one single prompt edited the entire video?

259
00:34:04,774 --> 00:34:10,505
Alex: What did the model actually do behind the scenes? Riley listed fifteen steps.

260
00:34:10,505 --> 00:34:12,268
Alex: Give us the highlights!

261
00:34:12,867 --> 00:34:18,802
Maya: He uploaded a raw video of himself talking, full of verbal stumbles and pauses.

262
00:34:18,802 --> 00:34:59,503
Maya: From that one prompt, the model analyzed the transcript, went onto X to find matching visual assets, cut out all the stumbles and dead air, built scenes inside a Blender template, took screenshots of its own work to self-check and fix mistakes, synced the camera angles and animations to every single word, added word-by-word captions, added sound effects and music, rendered three thousand four hundred eighty-seven frames in the cloud—which he claims took under twenty minutes and cost less than one dollar—and finally uploaded the finished video to Google Drive!

263
00:35:00,069 --> 00:35:10,322
Alex: Three thousand four hundred eighty-seven frames rendered in under twenty minutes for under a buck, plus self-checking its own Blender scenes via screenshots.

264
00:35:10,322 --> 00:35:13,193
Alex: Did Riley post anything else last night?

265
00:35:13,728 --> 00:35:23,253
Maya: Yes, he posted a short piece of advice for startups and content creators that got two hundred forty-three likes: "Don't be reverse-engineerable."

266
00:35:23,979 --> 00:35:37,880
Maya: 也就是说：现在已经有人用一条提示词跑完找素材、剪口误、搭场景、自查渲染的十五步视频流程，不到二十分钟就能出成片。

267
00:35:38,412 --> 00:35:40,562
Alex: Why is this on Fisher's radar today?

268
00:35:41,196 --> 00:35:47,199
Maya: Right now, The Daily Signal is strictly audio-only, and Fisher's other content is written articles.

269
00:35:47,199 --> 00:35:56,027
Maya: If Fisher ever wants to turn the podcast or his articles into short videos later on, workflows like Riley's are already being shared openly.

270
00:35:56,027 --> 00:36:06,973
Maya: Riley said he is releasing a full breakdown video later this week, so the notes say no need to write or reply—just keep an eye out for his full tutorial.

271
00:36:07,437 --> 00:36:17,616
Alex: So the takeaway is: keep our focus on audio and text today, and bookmark one-prompt video workflows for when we want video distribution later.

272
00:36:18,592 --> 00:36:28,158
Maya: 一句话：知道一句话剪视频已经能跑通全流程就行，等他这周发完整教程再按需了解。

273
00:36:30,208 --> 00:36:37,655
Alex: Now let's look at story nine, from Murat in Toronto—handle muratcan—who has about twenty-three thousand followers.

274
00:36:37,655 --> 00:36:45,102
Alex: His post only had two likes when we read it, which makes it a great quiet thread for a reply.

275
00:36:45,656 --> 00:36:49,394
Maya: He shared an experiment his team ran over the weekend.

276
00:36:49,394 --> 00:36:52,011
Maya: They were working on A I filmmaking.

277
00:36:52,011 --> 00:36:57,244
Maya: The question was how to keep characters looking the right way, with steady lighting.

278
00:36:57,884 --> 00:36:58,927
Alex: How did they solve it?

279
00:36:59,427 --> 00:37:05,546
Maya: They looked at how people building three-D animation engines for agents handle spatial layout.

280
00:37:05,546 --> 00:37:14,929
Maya: Before generating the video scene, they had the agent draw a top-down floor plan of where everyone stands and check it first.

281
00:37:14,929 --> 00:37:21,048
Maya: Eye lines, directions, and lighting came out much more stable than with text prompts alone.

282
00:37:21,659 --> 00:37:25,299
Alex: And what was Murat's big lesson from that weekend experiment?

283
00:37:25,884 --> 00:37:39,505
Maya: He called it "cross-profession transfer." When you are building with A I, borrowing a method that another industry already figured out is often much more effective than trying harder inside your own industry's habits.

284
00:37:40,198 --> 00:37:51,797
Maya: 简单说：用 A I 遇到卡点时，去借别的行业早就想明白的老办法，往往比在自己行业里死磕提示词管用得多。

285
00:37:52,373 --> 00:37:54,269
Alex: And why does that matter for Fisher?

286
00:37:54,913 --> 00:37:57,940
Maya: Because that is Fisher's exact signature angle!

287
00:37:57,940 --> 00:38:05,128
Maya: Fisher takes growth methods from more than ten years of growth work and brings them to A I products.

288
00:38:05,645 --> 00:38:31,146
Alex: For writing, the daily notes connect this to library item W M P five b—how classic Airbnb growth tactics translate to today's A I tools—with the theme: "The best methods in the A I era are often old methods from another industry." And we have a clean reply draft for Murat in Segment four using a general growth example.

289
00:38:31,676 --> 00:38:40,812
Maya: So the takeaway is: don't reinvent the wheel inside A I—import proven playbooks from older fields like growth and animation.

290
00:38:42,002 --> 00:38:49,809
Maya: 一句话：A I 时代最好用的招数，往往是从别的行业搬过来的成熟老办法。

291
00:38:51,859 --> 00:38:57,226
Alex: Story ten comes from staysaasy, an account with about seventeen thousand followers.

292
00:38:57,226 --> 00:39:03,487
Alex: It posted two sharp observations about the mood across the tech industry right now.

293
00:39:03,947 --> 00:39:10,861
Maya: The first post had a striking title: "Everyone sees opportunities and nobody is optimistic."

294
00:39:11,354 --> 00:39:17,736
Alex: Why would everyone see massive opportunities and still feel anxious and pessimistic?

295
00:39:18,369 --> 00:39:29,459
Maya: The post said the biggest difference between the A I wave and the earlier internet wave is that A I started with a massive conflict of interest from day one.

296
00:39:29,459 --> 00:39:35,374
Maya: Every company views the big foundation model labs as competitors that might eat their business alive.

297
00:39:35,374 --> 00:39:40,919
Maya: So anxiety is much higher, defense is much fiercer, and there are no real allies.

298
00:39:41,537 --> 00:39:45,056
Alex: And how did the post describe the opportunities people see?

299
00:39:45,528 --> 00:39:53,344
Maya: In the old days, people talked about seeing one to three opportunities that might give a ten-X return.

300
00:39:53,344 --> 00:40:02,804
Maya: Today, everyone sees fifty different opportunities that could be a thousand-X—except the probability of succeeding at any of them feels tiny!

301
00:40:02,804 --> 00:40:14,733
Maya: And the second post said every tech lead right now is building similar things: automated pull-request approvals, coding automation, A I cost optimization, and model switching and evaluation.

302
00:40:15,404 --> 00:40:27,936
Maya: 也就是说：现在人人都看得到几十个看似能翻千倍的机会，但大家手头做的事其实高度同质化，所以普遍焦虑多于乐观。

303
00:40:28,482 --> 00:40:36,978
Alex: Look at that contrast: everyone sees fifty shiny opportunities, and meanwhile everyone is building the exact same tooling.

304
00:40:36,978 --> 00:40:41,698
Alex: How does a growth veteran like Fisher look at that?

305
00:40:42,267 --> 00:40:45,999
Maya: In growth, having fifty ideas was never the bottleneck!

306
00:40:45,999 --> 00:40:48,487
Maya: Tools are available to everyone now.

307
00:40:48,487 --> 00:40:59,684
Maya: What separates winners from everyone else isn't spotting fifty opportunities—it's picking one single channel or direction and going all the way deep on it.

308
00:41:00,266 --> 00:41:07,848
Alex: The daily notes say skip writing a standalone post here, and use a growth view to reply to staysaasy.

309
00:41:08,359 --> 00:41:17,783
Maya: So the takeaway is: more opportunities do not mean more directions—pick one channel and go deep while everyone else chases fifty things.

310
00:41:19,126 --> 00:41:27,165
Maya: 一句话：机会多不等于方向多，找点子从来不难，难的是挑准一个渠道做透。

311
00:41:29,215 --> 00:41:38,661
Alex: Story twelve is a short, sharp Chinese post from sujingshen in Guangzhou, who has six thousand four hundred ninety-two followers.

312
00:41:38,661 --> 00:41:43,159
Alex: Yesterday we covered his product review of Tutti version two.

313
00:41:43,702 --> 00:41:52,923
Maya: Today he replied to someone about those super annoying mobile app splash-screen ads—specifically the "shake your phone" feature on splash-screen ads.

314
00:41:53,499 --> 00:41:59,495
Alex: Most phone users in China know these shake ads. What did sujingshen say is behind them?

315
00:42:00,072 --> 00:42:08,891
Maya: He said behind almost every "shake-to-open" splash screen sits a hard advertising revenue K P I—Key Performance Indicator.

316
00:42:08,891 --> 00:42:17,710
Maya: The moment management presses that revenue target down onto the team, user experience is always the first thing to give way.

317
00:42:18,297 --> 00:42:30,384
Maya: 简单说：开屏“摇一摇”这种招人烦的设计，背后都压着硬性的广告收入指标；指标一压下来，用户体验第一个让路。

318
00:42:30,846 --> 00:42:35,839
Alex: Anyone who has worked in growth knows this trade-off inside and out.

319
00:42:35,839 --> 00:42:45,441
Alex: When a company sets a short-term click or ad revenue metric as the ultimate target, teams will sacrifice user trust to hit the number.

320
00:42:45,973 --> 00:42:50,200
Maya: The daily notes say no need to write a standalone post here.

321
00:42:50,200 --> 00:43:01,120
Maya: Because we only do one Chinese reply a day and gave today's slot to vikingmute in Story eleven, sujingshen is listed as a backup in case Fisher prefers this topic.

322
00:43:01,723 --> 00:43:13,985
Alex: So the takeaway is: teams always optimize for the metric you judge them on—if you want better user experience, change the K P I instead of blaming the designers.

323
00:43:15,011 --> 00:43:23,436
